 "circuits": [],
 "equations": [],
 "operation": [
  {
   "topic": "Standard and resistance",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "The sensing element is platinum, and the part meets DIN EN 60751. R0 is its resistance at 0 °C: 100 Ω for Pt100 and 1000 Ω for Pt1000. Each comes in class F 0.3 (B) or F 0.6 (2B), both valid from -50 °C to +130 °C."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Measuring current",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "Drive a Pt100 with 0.3 to 1 mA and a Pt1000 with 0.1 to 0.3 mA. The current heats the chip by 0.4 K for each milliwatt it dissipates (at 0 °C), so that error has to be counted in the measurement."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Upper temperature",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "The rated range ends at +130 °C. On circuit board materials whose thermal expansion is matched to the chip, it can run up to +150 °C."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Soldering and mounting",
   "page": 3,
   "text": "The end terminations are galvanic tin over a nickel barrier. The chip mounts face up and is soldered by reflow, or by wave, for example a double wave of no more than 8 s at 235 °C."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Packaging and storage",
   "page": 3,
   "text": "Parts come face up on blister reels, 4000 to a reel; other packing is available on request. Stored as recommended they keep for at least 9 months from manufacture, and a nitrogen atmosphere is recommended for storage. Depending on how they were kept, the customer can requalify them for a longer shelf life."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Compliance",
   "page": 3,
   "text": "The part is RoHS compliant. It carries a California Proposition 65 warning for nickel."
  }
 ],
